<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François Fénelon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1651">1651</a>–<a href="/years/1715">1715</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Fénelon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François Fénelon was a French archbishop, theologian and writer. As tutor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s grandson, the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, he wrote The Adventures of Telemachus, whose criticism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> angered the king. Archbishop of Cambrai from 1695, he was confined to his diocese after his defence of Quietism was condemned by the pope in 1699.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-fenelon.jpg" alt="François Fénelon" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Vivien · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fran%C3%A7ois_de_Salignac_de_la_Mothe-F%C3%A9nelon.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Fénelon</b> was its most famous archbishop.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-cambrai">Prince-Bishopric of Cambrai</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château de Fénelon, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Cambrai, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.84,1.35&amp;year=1692" aria-label="Château de Fénelon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4582 579 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of François Fénelon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5032 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5032 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.84,1.35&amp;year=1692">What was here in 1692? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The world is not a phantom, it is the aggregate of all its families; and who can civilize and govern these with a nicer discrimination than women?&#8221;</p><footer>Treatise on the Education of Daughters (1687) &#183; tr. Thomas Frognall Dibdin, 1805 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/47621/pg47621-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Those who are instructed and busied in serious employments, have, in general, but a moderate curiosity.&#8221;</p><footer>Treatise on the Education of Daughters (1687) &#183; tr. Thomas Frognall Dibdin, 1805 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/47621/pg47621-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ignorance is one of the causes of the ennui and discontent of young persons, and of the absence of all rational amusement.&#8221;</p><footer>Treatise on the Education of Daughters (1687) &#183; tr. Thomas Frognall Dibdin, 1805 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/47621/pg47621-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
